# Rendering Complex Arrays in Laravel Blade Templates

The `htmlentities()` expects parameter 1 to be string, array given error is a classic symptom of attempting to render a complex data structure directly inside a Blade view without proper iteration or escaping logic. In your specific case, the controller successfully populates the view with a nested directory structure, but the blade template encounters issues because Laravel's default rendering mechanism tries to convert the entire array into a single HTML string for output.

When you call `{{ $watchFolder }}`, Blade attempts to escape and render the contents of the array as a whole. Since the data contains nested arrays and mixed types (files and subdirectories), the underlying PHP engine struggles to coerce this structure into a simple string representation suitable for immediate HTML injection, triggering the `htmlentities()` error. To resolve this, you must explicitly iterate over the array or use Laravel's built-in debugging helpers if the goal is inspection rather than display.

### Iterating Over Nested Directory Structures

Your controller logic uses a recursive function to traverse directories, resulting in an associative array where keys represent folder names and values contain either subfolder arrays or file lists. This structure is valid for database storage, but Blade requires explicit loops to render it meaningfully.

If you want to display the contents of `$watchFolder` as an HTML list, you need to write a recursive blade component or a standard loop that respects the nested nature of your data. Here is how you can approach rendering the top-level directories:

```blade
@foreach($watchFolder as $key => $item)
    <div class="folder-item">
        @if(is_array($item))
            <!-- This is a subdirectory containing files -->
            <h3>{{ ucfirst($key) }}</h3>
            <ul>
                @foreach($item as $subKey => $files)
                    <li>
                        @if(is_string($files))
                            <span>{{ $files }}</span>
                        @elseif(is_array($files))
                            <!-- Nested subfolder -->
                            <strong>{{ ucfirst($subKey) }}:</strong> 
                            <ul>
                                @foreach($files as $file)
                                    <li>{{ $file }}</li>
                                @endforeach
                            </ul>
                        @endif
                    </li>
                @endforeach
            </ul>
        @else
            <!-- This is a file at the root level -->
            <span>{{ $item }}</span>
        @endif
    </div>
@endforeach
```

This approach checks whether each value in the main array is itself an array (indicating a subdirectory) or a string (indicating a direct file). By nesting `@foreach` loops and using conditional rendering, you avoid trying to force a complex structure into a single line of output.

### Using Debug Helpers for Development

If your intention is simply to inspect the data during development rather than present it to end users, Laravel provides safer alternatives that bypass the strict HTML escaping requirements causing the error. You can use the `dd()` helper or the `dump()` function directly in the Blade file.

```blade
{{ dd($watchFolder) }}
{{-- or --}}
<x-debug::dump>{{ $watchFolder }}</x-debug::dump>
```

However, note that `dd()` will terminate the script execution after dumping the data, which is appropriate for debugging but not for production views. For production use, stick to explicit loops or helper classes designed to render tree structures cleanly.

### Handling Recursive Data Efficiently

Your current PHP implementation recursively builds the array structure, which works well for small datasets but can become memory-intensive with large directory trees. If you plan to scale this application, consider flattening the data in the controller before passing it to the view. You could pass a single flat array of objects containing `name`, `type` (file or directory), and `path` properties instead of deeply nested arrays. This simplifies the Blade template significantly, allowing you to render each item with a uniform loop without needing complex conditional logic inside the view.

